diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..48fb0b1
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,204 @@
+# Chat bots
+
+Any member can manage their bots via **Account settings → Set up chat bots**.
+Administrators can manage all bots. Bot codes are shown only on that management
+page; treat them like passwords.
+
+Bots are `User` records with the `bot` role, a creator, and a unique random
+`bot_code`. They reuse avatars, room membership, authorship, unread tracking,
+broadcasts, and push notifications. Bots do not join rooms by default. The
+**Add to all open rooms** checkbox on new/edit forms opts a bot into all existing
+and future open rooms (including rooms converted from closed to open). Turning it
+off keeps existing memberships. People still join open rooms automatically.
+Room member pickers allow selective bots to be added to open or private rooms;
+opted-in bots are automatic members of open rooms.
+
+Deletion deactivates the bot: its code immediately stops authenticating, its
+non-direct memberships are removed, and existing messages keep their author.
+The code stays reserved in the database and is never reassigned to a new bot.
+Bot role changes through account settings are intentionally prohibited.
+
+## Posting
+
+```sh
+curl --data-binary 'Deploy of v1.2.3 finished ✅' \
+  https://chat.example.com/rooms/ROOM_ID/BOT_CODE/messages
+```
+
+The endpoint authenticates only active bots; browser sessions are irrelevant.
+A bot must belong to the room. Missing and inaccessible rooms both return 404.
+The body is UTF-8 plain text, with HTML escaped and newlines preserved, regardless
+of the supplied content type. No JSON envelope or form field names are needed.
+
+Responses:
+
+- 201 with `Location: /messages/MESSAGE_ID` on success
+- 422 for blank or invalid UTF-8 content
+- 401 for invalid, banned, or deleted bot credentials
+- 404 for a missing or inaccessible room
+
+`BotMessageRequests` normalizes content types before Rack/Rails parsing so shell
+output is not interpreted as form data, JSON, or a method override. The endpoint
+uses the normal message creation callback and `broadcast_create` method, including
+push job scheduling.
+
+## Credential handling
+
+- The bot list is unpaginated, scoped by creator (or administrator), and marked
+  `no-store` in HTTP and Turbo caches.
+- Only name/avatar/webhook URL/open-room preference are permitted in management forms. Codes and ownership cannot
+  be set through these forms.
+- `bot_code` is filtered in Rails parameters and model inspection. Request-path
+  filtering also redacts the credential in Rails' initial request log.
+- **Serve over HTTPS. Configure reverse proxies, CDNs, APM/error collectors, and
+  access logs to redact `/rooms/:room_id/:bot_code/messages` URLs or exclude this
+  endpoint.** Rails filtering cannot protect logs outside Rails. Do not publish
+  these URLs in chat messages, screenshots, or CI logs.
+
+Run the migration with `bin/rails db:migrate` before deploying.
+
+Tests:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/controllers/bots_controller_test.rb test/controllers/bots/messages_controller_test.rb
+bin/rails test test/system/bots_test.rb
+```
+
+## Mention webhooks
+
+The new and edit bot forms accept an optional **Webhook URL** (HTTP or HTTPS).
+Saving an empty value removes it. Bots appear in the usual room-scoped mention
+picker, just like people; private rooms must explicitly include the bot.
+
+A new human-authored message queues one `Message::BotWebhookJob` for each distinct
+active, mentioned member bot with a webhook. No outbound HTTP runs in the posting
+request. Edits and bot-authored messages do not trigger callbacks (avoiding bot
+reply loops). Jobs recheck membership, active status, and webhook presence, and
+are discarded if the bot or source message has been deleted.
+
+The webhook receives a JSON POST with:
+
+- `event`: `mention`
+- `author`: the author's `id` and `name`
+- `room`: its `id`, `name`, and `reply_to` (the bot's authenticated posting path)
+- `message`: its `id`, `body.text`, `body.html`, and `link` (`/rooms/ID/@MESSAGE_ID`)
+
+Only that bot's Action Text mention attachments are removed from `body.text`;
+other mentions and text are preserved. `body.html` is the full rendered rich text.
+Both paths are relative to the Campfire origin. Treat `reply_to` as a secret.
+
+HTTP 200 with `text/plain` or `text/html` posts a reply as the bot and broadcasts
+it normally. Plain text is escaped with newlines preserved; HTML uses Action
+Text's normal sanitization. Content-type charset parameters are supported; reply
+bodies must be UTF-8. HTTP 204, other statuses/types, empty bodies, invalid UTF-8,
+and replies over 1 MiB produce no message. The bot can instead use `reply_to`
+later with the plain-text posting API documented above.
+
+Delivery uses the existing private-network guard and pins the resolved public IP.
+Redirects are not followed, so the bot's posting credential is never forwarded to
+another destination. Open/read/write timeouts are bounded. HTTP errors are logged
+without URLs, response bodies, or exception messages, and are not automatically
+retried (a retry could execute the bot's command twice). Webhook URLs are filtered
+from Rails parameters and model inspection since they may carry secrets.
+
+Production requires the normal Resque worker to process webhook jobs.
+
+Regression tests:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/models/message/bot_webhook_test.rb test/controllers/bots_controller_test.rb test/controllers/autocompletable/users_controller_test.rb test/controllers/bots/messages_controller_test.rb
+```
+
+## Joined webhooks
+
+When a bot first becomes a member of a non-direct room, its webhook receives:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "joined",
+  "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/42-Xy3abc9defGh/messages" }
+}
+```
+
+The same HTTP 200 text/HTML reply handling and network protections as mention
+webhooks apply. Direct conversations never send `joined`.
+
+Membership creation records a `Room::BotJoin` within the transaction, then queues
+`Room::BotWebhookJob` after commit. A unique bot/room index prevents repeated grants,
+preference toggles, or removing and re-adding a bot from sending another join event.
+The record intentionally outlives membership; it is deleted with the bot or room.
+People retain the bulk-insert membership path; bot grants use normal model creation
+so that automatic and manual additions share the same callback.
+
+Existing memberships are not replayed on deployment. Adding a webhook later does
+not replay past joins, and bots without webhooks or with inactive status do not
+queue deliveries. Like mention webhooks, delivery rechecks access and does not
+retry HTTP failures. This is once-per-room event scheduling, not a guarantee of
+successful delivery across network or worker failures.
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/models/user/bot_rooms_test.rb test/models/room/bot_webhook_test.rb
+```
+
+## Per-member bot limit
+
+Administrators configure **Bots per member** on `/account/edit`. This uses the
+account's JSON settings, with a default of 5 for both existing and new accounts;
+no data migration is needed. Zero blocks all new member-owned bots. Administrators
+are exempt from the limit.
+
+The quota counts each creator's non-deactivated bots. Deleting a bot frees a slot;
+lowering the limit never removes or prevents editing existing bots. The bots
+page hides **Add a chat bot** at or above the limit, and a creation-only model
+validation also rejects direct submissions (including forms opened before the
+limit changed). The check uses the bot's creator, not the current session user.
+
+Regression tests:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/models/user/bot_limit_test.rb test/models/account_test.rb test/controllers/bots_controller_test.rb test/controllers/accounts_controller_test.rb
+bin/rails test test/system/bots_test.rb
+```
+
+## Automatic bot suspension
+
+The fixed safety limits live in `User::BotSafety`; they are not account settings.
+Run `bin/rails db:migrate` to add the persistent safety state.
+
+- Each bot may post 10 messages in a rolling 10-second window, shared across all
+  rooms, API posts, and both types of webhook reply. The next attempt is rejected
+  and starts a 30-second cooldown. Rejected API requests receive `429` and an
+  integer `Retry-After` rounded up to the seconds remaining. Webhook replies are
+  discarded, not deferred. Five further attempts in that cooldown suspend the
+  bot; the triggering API request and subsequent requests receive `403`.
+- Any non-2xx webhook response or transport failure increments a shared delivery
+  failure streak. Every 2xx response resets it, including responses that do not
+  contain a usable reply. Ten consecutive failures suspend the bot. Deliveries
+  are not retried automatically.
+- Suspension is distinct from deletion/deactivation: rooms, messages, credentials,
+  ownership, and the owner's occupied quota slot remain intact. Both owners and
+  administrators see **Suspended** and its reason on the bots page. Only an
+  administrator sees **Reactivate**, with Turbo's native browser confirmation;
+  the reactivation endpoint also enforces administrator authorization.
+- Reactivation clears all safety counters, posting timestamps, cooldown, and
+  reason. Editing bot settings never clears them. Reactivating an active or
+  deleted bot is a no-op, so that endpoint cannot bypass an active cooldown.
+
+Posting timestamps (at most ten), cooldown attempts, and delivery failures live
+on the user row rather than in a process-local/cache counter. `with_lock` encloses
+posting admission and message creation together; SQLite uses the configured
+immediate transactions. Posting history survives message deletion. Rejection
+exceptions are raised only after the safety-state transaction commits.
+
+Webhook HTTP requests run outside database locks. Jobs recheck active status
+before sending, and both reply eligibility and posting admission are rechecked
+after HTTP completes. An already-sent request cannot be recalled by suspension,
+but its reply cannot post while the bot is suspended. Delivery streaks are
+recorded in completion order.
+
+Regression tests:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/controllers/bots test/models/user/bot_safety_test.rb test/models/user/bot_webhook_safety_test.rb
+bin/rails test test/system/bots_test.rb
+```
diff --git a/lib/rails_ext/bot_message_requests.rb b/lib/rails_ext/bot_message_requests.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..310e8ee
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/rails_ext/bot_message_requests.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+# This endpoint accepts a raw text body even when curl supplies a form content
+# type. Normalize it before Rack's method override or Rails parameter parsing.
+class BotMessageRequests
+  PATH = %r{\A(/rooms/[^/]+/)[^/]+(/messages)/?\z}
+
+  def initialize(app)
+    @app = app
+  end
+
+  def call(env)
+    if env["REQUEST_METHOD"] == "POST" && PATH.match?(env["PATH_INFO"])
+      env["CONTENT_TYPE"] = "text/plain; charset=utf-8"
+    end
+
+    @app.call(env)
+  end
+
+  # Rails' standard parameter filtering handles query/body parameters, but not
+  # credentials in path segments (including the initial "Started POST" log).
+  module FilteredPath
+    def filtered_path
+      super.sub(%r{\A(/rooms/[^/]+/)[^/]+(/messages)(?=/|\?|\z)}, '\1[FILTERED]\2')
+    end
+  end
+end
